(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-acid-base-part-1-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Acid-Base Part I Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 6","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 24-year-old man with a panic attack is breathing fast, with perioral numbness and tingling hands. His roommate hands him a paper bag to breathe into. What is accurate education about the bag?","choices":["It is unnecessary and may lower his oxygen","It safely corrects his carbon dioxide","It is needed to prevent tetany","It works by raising his bicarbonate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Breathing into a paper bag is not necessary for hyperventilation: it fails to correct the carbon dioxide and may decrease oxygen, and any benefit is placebo. Hyperventilation is usually self-limited, and the cause is treated. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has resistant hypertension, muscle weakness and a potassium of 2.8 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L). Arterial blood gas: pH 7.49 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 47 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 34 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder is present?","choices":["Respiratory acidosis","Metabolic acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Respiratory alkalosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The pH of 7.49 is alkalemia, and the bicarbonate of 34 is high and matches it, so it is metabolic. The carbon dioxide of 47 is slight respiratory compensation. Hypertension with hypokalemia points to a hypertensive saline-unresponsive cause such as primary aldosteronism. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man has a pH of 7.30 (normal 7.35 to 7.45) and a carbon dioxide of 52 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg). To decide whether this respiratory problem is acute or chronic, which value is compared with its expected level?","choices":["Bicarbonate","Carbon dioxide","Anion gap","Urine chloride"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. For a respiratory disorder, compensation is renal, so the expected bicarbonate is checked. A small rise (about 1 per 10 mm Hg) means acute, and a large rise (about 3.5 per 10) means chronic. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman who moved to a high-altitude town months ago has a chronic respiratory alkalosis. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.47 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 27 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 19 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which serum electrolyte pattern accompanies her renal compensation?","choices":["High chloride and low potassium","Low chloride and high bicarbonate","High potassium and low chloride","Normal chloride with a high anion gap"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In respiratory alkalosis the kidneys excrete bicarbonate and retain chloride, giving a reduced bicarbonate, hyperchloremia and hypokalemia (cells also secrete hydrogen ions in exchange for potassium). Her bicarbonate of 19 shows that compensation. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man arrived at a high-altitude camp two hours ago and is hyperventilating; his carbon dioxide is 25 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg). Using an acute bicarbonate fall of about 2 mEq/L per 10 mm Hg fall in carbon dioxide, from midpoints of 40 and 24, what bicarbonate is expected?","choices":["16.5 mEq/L","21 mEq/L","24 mEq/L","27 mEq/L"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Carbon dioxide fell 15 (40 to 25); acutely bicarbonate falls about 2 per 10, so 24 - 3 = 21 mEq/L. Two hours is acute; altitude is a listed hypoxic cause of hyperventilation. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 19-year-old man with type 1 diabetes presents in diabetic ketoacidosis. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.19 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 19 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 7 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); potassium 5.9 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L). What explains his high potassium?","choices":["Potassium enters cells as hydrogen ions leave","High pH binds more calcium to albumin","Kidneys excrete chloride and retain potassium","Hydrogen ions enter cells as potassium leaves"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In acidosis, hydrogen ions shift from the extracellular fluid into cells and potassium shifts out of cells in exchange, raising the serum potassium. His pH of 7.19 is a marked acidemia, so hyperkalemia is expected. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man has hypertension that is hard to control and muscle weakness. He takes no diuretics, has not vomited, and eats a bag of black licorice every day. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.47 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 47 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 33 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); potassium 3.0 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L); urine chloride is high. Which part of his history best accounts for this blood gas?","choices":["Antacid use","Anxiety with hyperventilation","His daily black licorice","Hidden vomiting"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. High pH with a high bicarbonate is metabolic alkalosis; the high urine chloride makes it saline unresponsive, and his high blood pressure puts it in the hypertensive group, which lists licorice. Low potassium with weakness fits. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman with a metabolic alkalosis from repeated vomiting has generalized weakness and hyporeflexia. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.52 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 48 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 38 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which laboratory finding best explains her weakness and hyporeflexia?","choices":["Low bicarbonate","Low potassium","High potassium","High carbon dioxide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Metabolic alkalosis brings hypokalemia (potassium shifts into cells, and vomiting adds losses), and the associated hypokalemia causes weakness and hyporeflexia. Her alkalemia at 7.52 fits. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with cirrhosis and hepatic failure also has type 2 diabetes treated with metformin and takes an antacid for heartburn. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.48 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 31 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 23 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which of his conditions best accounts for this blood gas?","choices":["His hepatic failure","His metformin","His antacid","Ketoacidosis from his diabetes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. High pH with a low carbon dioxide of 31 and a nearly normal bicarbonate is respiratory alkalosis. Hepatic failure is a listed cause of respiratory alkalosis; neither of his medicines lowers carbon dioxide. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 23-year-old woman has a panic attack before an exam, with light-headedness, perioral numbness and paresthesias (tingling) in both hands. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.53 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 27 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 22 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder is present?","choices":["Metabolic alkalosis","Respiratory acidosis","Acute respiratory alkalosis","Chronic respiratory alkalosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The pH of 7.53 is alkalemia, and the carbon dioxide of 27 is low and matches it, so it is respiratory. Bicarbonate fell about 2 per 10 mm Hg (13 below 40 gives about 22), the acute response. Anxiety-driven hyperventilation with perioral numbness fits. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman with a bicarbonate of 9 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L) from ethylene glycol ingestion has a carbon dioxide of 15 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg). By the Winter formula, what does her carbon dioxide show?","choices":["Impending respiratory failure","An added respiratory alkalosis","Appropriate compensation","An added respiratory acidosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Winter formula: 1.5 x 9 + 8 = 21.5, plus or minus 2, so about 19.5 to 23.5. Her 15 is below that range, which is more lowering than compensation explains: an added respiratory alkalosis, a second primary disorder. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man after a motor vehicle crash has a flail chest (a chest wall segment moving paradoxically) and shallow breathing. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.27 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 56 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 25 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which process explains this blood gas?","choices":["Lactic acidosis from blood loss","Chronic hypoventilation","Hypoventilation from the chest injury","Hyperventilation from pain and anxiety"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A high carbon dioxide of 56 matching a low pH, with a bicarbonate barely raised, is acute respiratory acidosis. Thoracic trauma, including flail chest and pneumothorax, is a listed acute cause. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with a body mass index of 58 has loud snoring, witnessed pauses in breathing at night and daytime sleepiness. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.34 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 55 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 30 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder is present?","choices":["Metabolic alkalosis","Respiratory alkalosis","Chronic respiratory acidosis","Acute respiratory acidosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The pH of 7.34 is just below normal, with a carbon dioxide of 55 that is high and matches it. Bicarbonate is 30, a rise of about 5 (1.5 x 3.5), the size of chronic compensation. Obesity, sleep disordered breathing and chronic hypercapnia form the obesity hypoventilation triad. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with septic shock has a lactate elevation. Sodium 132 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), potassium 4.0 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L), chloride 96 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L), bicarbonate 12 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). What is her anion gap?","choices":["24","28","36","48"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Anion gap = 132 - (96 + 12) = 132 - 108 = 24, above 9 to 15: elevated. Lactic acidosis, the L of the mnemonic, raises the gap through unmeasured anions. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with chronic lung disease is drowsy and confused, with asterixis (a flapping tremor of the outstretched hands). Arterial blood gas: pH 7.22 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 75 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 30 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder best explains his findings?","choices":["Respiratory alkalosis","Respiratory acidosis","Metabolic acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The pH of 7.22 is acidemia, and the carbon dioxide of 75 is high and matches it, so it is respiratory acidosis. Bicarbonate of 30 is the renal response. Somnolence, confusion and asterixis are its listed clinical findings, and chronic lung disease fits. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man has hypertension treated with a thiazide diuretic, stable chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, and osteoarthritis for which he takes aspirin. He feels weak. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.48 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 46 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 33 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); potassium 3.1 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L); urine chloride is low. Which part of his history best accounts for this blood gas?","choices":["His chronic lung disease","His aspirin","Primary aldosteronism","His thiazide diuretic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. High pH with a high bicarbonate is metabolic alkalosis, and the low urine chloride marks the saline-responsive type. Diuretic therapy is a saline-responsive cause, and the low potassium and weakness fit; the carbon dioxide of 46 is the expected compensation. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man is found unresponsive at a party with pinpoint pupils and a respiratory rate of 6. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.24 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 62 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 26 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder is present?","choices":["Respiratory alkalosis","Acute respiratory acidosis","Chronic respiratory acidosis","Metabolic acidosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The pH of 7.24 is acidemia, and the carbon dioxide of 62 is high, which matches it, so the problem is respiratory. Bicarbonate rose only about 1 per 10 mm Hg (22 above 40 gives about 26), the acute response. A slow rate of 6 fits central nervous system depression. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man who ingested ethylene glycol has an arterial pH of 6.62 (normal 7.35 to 7.45). Which complications does this pH threaten?","choices":["Reduced oxygen release from hemoglobin","Seizures, cerebral edema, cardiac irritability","Tetany from low ionized calcium","Hypokalemia from potassium entering cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A pH below 6.7 is unsustainable and may cause cardiac hypocontractility and irritability, seizures, cerebral edema and metabolic disturbances; it may warrant therapy to normalize the pH along with treating the cause. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man has a metabolic alkalosis with a high urine chloride. He takes no diuretics and has not vomited. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.48 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 47 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 34 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which finding should be checked next to narrow the cause?","choices":["His expected carbon dioxide by Winter","His blood pressure","His anion gap","His urine chloride again"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A high urine chloride (above 40 mEq/L) means saline-unresponsive alkalosis, whose causes split by blood pressure: normotensive (severe potassium depletion, hypoparathyroidism and hypercalcemia) or hypertensive (primary aldosteronism, mineralocorticoids, licorice). — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman develops an acute respiratory acidosis after aspiration pneumonitis two hours ago. When will her kidneys begin to compensate?","choices":["In 6 to 12 hours, completing over days","Within minutes, completing in an hour","After weeks, completing over months","They do not; the lungs compensate instead"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In respiratory acidosis the kidneys excrete acid as ammonium chloride and retain bicarbonate; this begins 6 to 12 hours after onset, and complete compensation may take days. At two hours her bicarbonate is near normal. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman with long-standing chronic obstructive pulmonary disease comes for a routine visit, at her baseline. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.34 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 58 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 32 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder is present?","choices":["Chronic respiratory acidosis","Acute respiratory acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her carbon dioxide of 58 is high and her pH of 7.34 is just below normal, so the process is respiratory acidosis. Bicarbonate is 32, a rise of about 6 (1.8 x 3.5), the size of chronic compensation. Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is a classic chronic cause. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has glaucoma treated with an oral carbonic anhydrase inhibitor, type 2 diabetes treated with metformin, and daily aspirin use for headaches. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.32 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 30 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 15 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 140 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 115 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). Which of her medicines best accounts for this blood gas?","choices":["Metformin","Aspirin","A respiratory cause, not a medicine","The carbonic anhydrase inhibitor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Low pH and low bicarbonate make a metabolic acidosis; the gap is 140 - (115 + 15) = 10, within 9 to 15, with a high chloride: a normal-gap, hyperchloremic acidosis. Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors are on the normal-gap list (loss of bicarbonate). — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman who overdosed on aspirin (a salicylate) has a bicarbonate of 14 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L) and a carbon dioxide of 20 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg). By the Winter formula, what does her carbon dioxide show?","choices":["An added respiratory acidosis","Impending respiratory failure","An added respiratory alkalosis","Appropriate compensation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Winter: 1.5 x 14 + 8 = 29, plus or minus 2, so 27 to 31. Her 20 is below that: a second, respiratory alkalosis on top of the metabolic acidosis. Salicylates appear on both lists, elevated-gap acidosis and toxic respiratory alkalosis. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man with Guillain-Barré syndrome has worsening weakness and shallow breathing. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.26 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 58 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 26 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). What is the priority treatment for his acid-base disorder?","choices":["Give sodium bicarbonate to raise the pH","Wait for renal compensation","Careful oxygen titration alone","Restore adequate ventilation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A high carbon dioxide with a low pH and a barely raised bicarbonate is acute respiratory acidosis, and Guillain-Barré syndrome is a listed neuromuscular cause. Acute respiratory acidosis is treated by restoring ventilation while the cause is treated. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with type 2 diabetes treated with metformin received several liters of normal saline during abdominal surgery. He has had no diarrhea. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.33 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 33 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 17 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 141 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 114 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). Which explains this blood gas?","choices":["His metformin","Diabetic ketoacidosis","Postoperative hypoventilation","The large saline infusion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Low pH with a low bicarbonate is metabolic acidosis; the gap is 141 - (114 + 17) = 10, within 9 to 15, with a high chloride: a normal-gap, hyperchloremic acidosis. Saline infusion is a listed normal-gap cause (chloride retention). — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 48-year-old woman five days after hip replacement develops sudden pleuritic chest pain and fast breathing. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.51 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 30 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 23 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); partial pressure of oxygen 68 mm Hg (normal 80 to 100 mm Hg). Which process best accounts for this blood gas?","choices":["Hypoventilation from opioid pain medicine","Diuretic-induced metabolic alkalosis","Obesity hypoventilation syndrome","Hyperventilation from a pulmonary embolism"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. High pH with a low carbon dioxide of 30 is respiratory alkalosis, and her bicarbonate of 23 has barely moved (acute). Pulmonary embolism is a listed pulmonary cause; after surgery, with sudden pleuritic pain and a low oxygen, it fits. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man who drank methanol is obtunded. Arterial blood gas: pH 6.65 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 18 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 2 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Besides treating the poisoning, what does this pH warrant?","choices":["Waiting for respiratory compensation","Therapy to raise the serum pH","No pH-directed therapy","Paper bag rebreathing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A pH below 6.7 is unsustainable: it may cause cardiac hypocontractility and irritability, seizures and cerebral edema, and may warrant therapy to normalize the serum pH along with treatment of the underlying cause. His pH is 6.65. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 22-year-old college athlete with gastroenteritis has vomited for three days and is dizzy on standing. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.51 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 48 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 37 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); potassium 3.1 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L); urine chloride is low. In addition to treating the cause, which therapy is indicated?","choices":["A sodium bicarbonate infusion","Paper bag rebreathing","Starting a thiazide diuretic","Isotonic saline"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Vomiting (loss of hydrochloric acid) with orthostatic dizziness and a low urine chloride is saline-responsive metabolic alkalosis, a sign of extracellular volume contraction. Isotonic saline corrects that volume deficit. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has had respiratory acidosis for several days, and his carbon dioxide is 60 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg). Using bicarbonate rises of about 1 mEq/L per 10 mm Hg acutely and about 3.5 per 10 chronically, from a midpoint of 24, which bicarbonate fits chronic compensation?","choices":["24 mEq/L","26 mEq/L","31 mEq/L","38 mEq/L"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Carbon dioxide rose 20 mm Hg (40 to 60). Chronically bicarbonate rises about 3.5 per 10 mm Hg, so 24 + 7 = 31; acutely it would be only 24 + 2 = 26. Renal compensation takes days to complete, and he has had several days. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 48-year-old woman with metabolic alkalosis from diuretics has a bicarbonate of 32 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L) (8 above the working midpoint of 24). Using a carbon dioxide rise of about 0.75 mm Hg per 1 mEq/L rise in bicarbonate, from a midpoint of 40, what carbon dioxide is expected?","choices":["34 mm Hg","40 mm Hg","46 mm Hg","52 mm Hg"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bicarbonate rose 8 (24 to 32), and 8 x 0.75 = 6, so the expected carbon dioxide is 40 + 6 = 46 mm Hg. That is well under the 55 above which a concurrent respiratory acidosis is suspected. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 59"}]};
